---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { doc, code, REPO } from '../site';
import { failures } from '../data/failures';

// The rules the code is built to, each with its proof. Source: the repo's
// docs/ENGINEERING.md, which moves here (site-structure-2026-10-29.md §3).
const rules = [
  {
    title: 'A billing period is billed once.',
    body: 'Before closing a billing period, Hardcount locks the subscription and checks that the period has not been closed already. Behind that, the database refuses a second invoice for the same period (a unique index). So a crash, or two copies of Hardcount working at once, cannot bill it twice.',
    proof: 'A billing process was killed at five different points, and left 0 duplicate and 0 lost invoices. To show the test can catch a failure, it was run again with the unique index removed: four billing processes working on the same 40 periods made 103 invoices. That run was made before the subscription lock was added; the lock is now a second guard in front of the index.',
    link: ['Correctness under failure', '/docs/internals/benchmarks/failure-correctness/'],
  },
  {
    title: 'Fail loudly.',
    body: 'If Hardcount cannot read something a bill needs, that bill stops with an error. It never fills in a default and bills that. After eight failed attempts, the subscription is parked: nothing retries it until you look at it and retry.',
    proof: 'How it works, and why, is in its design record.',
    link: ['Design record 119', '/docs/internals/decisions/119-billing-failure-record-keyed-to-the-due-instant/'],
  },
  {
    title: 'For card charges, Stripe’s record decides what was paid.',
    body: 'A charge with an unclear result is held while Hardcount checks Stripe for the payment. If Stripe shows none 24 hours after the attempt, the invoice is charged again.',
    proof: 'Each case, and the known gaps, are listed in one place.',
    link: ['Card payment failure handling', doc('payments.md')],
  },
  {
    title: 'One Postgres.',
    body: 'All billing data lives in Postgres; Redis is for rate limits only. No queue broker, warehouse or workflow engine until a measurement says Postgres is not enough.',
    proof: 'On a large database server (64 GB memory) with default Postgres settings, Hardcount took 12,000 usage events a second: it passed 4 of 5 ten-minute runs in one series and 5 of 5 in another. Changing one Postgres setting removed the slow spikes: the worst p99 over 10 seconds (the time 99 of 100 requests finish within) fell from 177 ms to 52 ms. The page gives the hardware and method.',
    link: ['How much it handles', doc('scale.md')],
  },
];

const checks = [
  ['Accounts kept apart', 'Tests check the Postgres row-level security policies. Code that skips them fails a test until someone adds it to a list of allowed exceptions.', code('internal/arch/txbypass_allowlist_test.go')],
  ['One charge per invoice', 'When two workers race to charge an invoice, exactly one wins.', code('internal/invoice/auto_charge_claim_integration_test.go')],
  ['A clean 13-month run', 'A test bills 13 months in a row, and after each month a read-only checker finds no inconsistent data.', code('internal/api/clock_soak_e2e_test.go')],
  ['Generated cases for the money math', 'Property tests, which check a rule against many generated inputs, cover billing dates, pricing, proration, tax and the order credits are used in.', code('internal/domain/pricing_property_test.go')],
  ['Every doc command runs', 'CI runs every command in “Send usage, get an invoice” against a real build.', doc('first-invoice.md')],
];
---
<Base
  title="Reliability: how Hardcount keeps a bill right"
  description="What can go wrong in usage billing, what Hardcount does about each case, how it is tested, and the rare cases still left."
>
  <section class="top">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1>How Hardcount keeps a bill right</h1>
      <p class="lede">A mistake in a bill costs someone real money. So Hardcount follows a few fixed rules, listed below, and each one links to what backs it up, such as a test result. Where a case is not fully handled yet, this page says so.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="cases">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What can go wrong, and what Hardcount does</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        {failures.map((c) => (
          <article class="card" id={c.id}>
            <h3>“{c.title}”</h3>
            <p>{c.body}</p>
            {c.left && (
              <div class="gap">
                <p class="gap-label">The rare case, and what happens</p>
                <p>{c.left}</p>
              </div>
            )}
            <a href={c.link.href}>{c.link.label} →</a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="rules">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The rules the code follows</h2>
      {rules.map((r) => (
        <div class="rule">
          <h3>{r.title}</h3>
          <p>{r.body}</p>
          <p class="proof"><strong>Evidence:</strong> {r.proof} <a href={r.link[1]}>{r.link[0]} →</a></p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tested">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>How it is tested</h2>
      <p>Every change ships with a test at the right layer: Go tests against a real Postgres for money and state, and browser tests for the dashboard. A fix is proven by watching its test fail without it. These run on every change:</p>
      <ul class="checks">
        {checks.map(([t, b, href]) => <li><strong>{t}.</strong> {b} <a href={href}>See it →</a></li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="small"><a href="/docs/internals/testing/">How we test →</a> · <a href="/docs/internals/benchmarks/sustained-throughput/">Sustained throughput →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="built">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>How it is built</h2>
      <p>One Go program runs the API and the background jobs. When you run several copies, each background job runs on only one copy at a time. Postgres row-level security keeps accounts apart on most requests. Every decision, including the reversed ones, is written down.</p>
      <p class="small"><a href="/docs/internals/architecture/">Architecture →</a> · <a href="/docs/internals/decisions/">Design decisions →</a> · <a href={`${REPO}`}>The code →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="security">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>Accounts kept apart in the database, personal data and keys encrypted at rest, signed webhooks, and a private way to report a vulnerability.</p>
      <p class="small"><a href="/docs/project/security/">Security policy →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .top { padding-top: 72px; }
  .lede { font-size: 20px; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 18px; margin-top: 24px; }
  .card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; scroll-margin-top: 24px; }
  .card h3 { font-size: 20px; }
  .card p { font-size: 16px; }
  .card > a { margin-top: auto; font-size: 15px; }
  .gap { background: var(--paper-2); border-left: 3px solid var(--copper); border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 14px; margin: 4px 0 18px; }
  .gap p { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 15px; }
  .gap .gap-label { font-weight: 600; color: var(--copper-ink); font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .rule { margin-bottom: 28px; }
  .rule h3 { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .proof { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 16px; }
  .checks li { margin-bottom: 10px; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
